</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Evaluation Strategy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each dataset contains a unique set of text <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">items</em>; labels between pairs indicate which item is more extreme on the target scalar construct (e.g., which of two campaign ad transcripts is more negative about an opponent).
Therefore, the pairwise labeled data in each dataset form a directed connected graph <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices representing text items and edges representing comparison relations.<span id="footnote12" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">12</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            This structure is unlike standard LLM paired preference datasets, which are disconnected. There, comparisons between generated texts are conditioned on some shared context, like an instruction, making them incomparable across contexts.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The graphs are connected, so constructing a train–test split and an adequate evaluation strategy is a somewhat delicate operation.
We proceed as follows.
First sample <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{test}}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{test}}=100</annotation></semantics></math> high-degree held-out evaluation vertices <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{test}}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{test}}\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, with train vertices <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{train}}\coloneqq V\setminus V_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{train}}\coloneqq V\setminus V_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The induced subgraph <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\text{train}}\coloneqq G\left[V_{\text{train}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\text{train}}\coloneqq G\left[V_{\text{train}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the edges and items used for training the finetuned models.
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\text{eval}}\coloneqq G\left[E\setminus E_{\text{train}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>eval</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\text{eval}}\coloneqq G\left[E\setminus E_{\text{train}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is then the graph of all edges that contain at least one test vertex <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the graph also contains items from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation metrics.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary focus is on LLMs’ <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scoring performance</em>.
To estimate items’ ground-truth reference scores, we fit a Bradley-Terry (BT) model<span id="footnote13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">13</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              Using the <span id="footnote13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">choix</span> implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maystre et al., 2022</a>)</cite></span></span></span> <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">to the entire graph</em> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in item-level scores <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{BT}}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>BT</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{BT}}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in V</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then evaluate how well an LLM can predict these item-level scores.
Specifically, we measure scoring performance with <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spearman rank correlation</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> and the root mean squared error (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RMSE</span>) in the subset of items in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote14" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">14</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              This avoids leakage when evaluating finetuned models and makes evaluations of prompted and finetuned models comparable.
</span></span></span>
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> measures how well an LLM ranks text items relative to the reference scores.
RMSE measures the average magnitude of the errors between text items’ predicted and reference scores. These metrics are complementary: <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> focuses on ranking consistency, while RMSE evaluates the precision of the numerical predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A secondary focus is on <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pair classification performance</em>, i.e., whether models can predict the pairwise labels <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{I}[x_{i}&gt;x_{j}],\ (x_{i},x_{j})\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[x_{i}&gt;x_{j}],\ (x_{i},x_{j})\in E</annotation></semantics></math>.
We measure classification performance with <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">accuracy</span>, which we compute against <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> edges in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\text{eval}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>eval</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\text{eval}}</annotation></semantics></math>---that is, any edge where a test vertex appears.<span id="footnote15" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">15</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              This choice biases the finetuned accuracies upward because they have seen the train vertices, but there are relatively few edges with both <math id="footnote15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Influence of training data size.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the finetuning experiments, we also evaluate the effect of increasing the number of edges, while controlling for differences in dataset structure (<a href="#S3.T1" title="In Grandstanding ‣ 3.1 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Our algorithm iteratively adds edges to each of the three graphs such that the average degree and clustering coefficient remain the same for each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, up until about 2000 edges, where maintaining graph similarity is no longer feasible given the differing structural characteristics of our datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we report results from prompting and then fine-tuning.
Overall, pairwise comparison does not improve prompting results (see <a href="#S3.T2" title="In Finetuning. ‣ 3.2 Methods and Models ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), but it does help in finetuning (<a href="#A3.F10" title="In Appendix C Additional Results ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Prompting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Finetuning. ‣ 3.2 Methods and Models ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares pairwise and pointwise prompting approaches for text scoring.
We report results for both zero- and 5-shot prompting with probability-weighted averaging.<span id="footnote16" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">16</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Using probability-weighted averages instead of models’ modal response tends to improve the accuracy and scoring performance
for all models and few-shot settings in the <span id="footnote16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Grandstanding</span> data and the <span id="footnote16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Ad-Negativity</span> data (but one)
and
for most models and most few-shot settings in the <span id="footnote16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Immigration Fear</span> data for accuracy and RMSE.
</span></span></span>
We summarize our main insights below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Reward model finetuning results by model, number of training examples, and dataset. Finetuning tends to outperform prompting after roughly 2,000 examples. <em id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Notes:</em> Metrics reported are the pair classification accuracy (Acc), as well as the Spearman’s rank correlation (<math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) and the root mean squared error (RMSE) against the ground-truth BT scores. Values report are averages <math id="S4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> one standard deviation computed by summarizing results across five folds.</figcaption>
