<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Methodological details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>The Bradley-Terry model</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Bradley–Terry model turns pairwise comparisons into a single latent scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let the items (e.g., texts) in a set of pairwise comparisons be indexed by <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Associate each item <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with a positive “strength” parameters <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In our applications, these parameters estimate items’ locations on the underlying (unobserved) latent dimension that represents the scalar construct under study (e.g., ad negativity).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, in a pairwise comparison of <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the Bradley-Terry model models the probability that <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> “beats” <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of strength (equivalently, is “higher” on the underlying dimensions) as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(i\succ j)=\frac{\alpha_{i}}{\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≻</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(i\succ j)=\frac{\alpha_{i}}{\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, writing <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\equiv\exp(\lambda_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\equiv\exp(\lambda_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, the log odds of <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> being chosen over <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is the following.</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{logit}\left(\Pr(i\succ j)\right)=\log\left[\frac{\Pr(i\succ j)}{\Pr(j\succ i)}\right]=\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>logit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≻</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≻</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≻</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{logit}\left(\Pr(i\succ j)\right)=\log\left[\frac{\Pr(i\succ j)}{\Pr(j\succ i)}\right]=\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a latent propensity relevant to the comparison, such as “ability,” “preference,” etc., depending on the context.
The outcomes of pairwise comparisons are used to estimate these latent propensities relative to a chosen reference.
For identification, one can either choose a reference item or impose a constraint such as <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">After estimation, the parameters may be rescaled (for example, to the unit interval) without affecting the fitted probabilities.
Probabilities are typically estimated using maximum likelihood, assuming independence of all pairwise comparisons.
Bias-reduced or penalized likelihood methods are often used to handle small samples or quasi- or complete separation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Pairwise debiasing</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our pairwise debiasing strategy borrows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> by prompting the model with different orderings of items in pairs to avoid positional biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our pairwise prompts first explain the annotation task that define the criterion for comparison (e.g., emotional intensity) and then present a pair of text items in separate lines prefixed by “Text 1” and “Text 2.”
The LLM is tasked to indicate which of the texts meets the comparison criterion and to respond either with “1” or “2.”</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Illustration of our pair augmentation strategy.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">position in prompt</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">swap label</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">reverse order</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">first option</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">second option</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><em id="A1.T4.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">original pair</em></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">no</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">no</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Text 1: “item 1”</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Text 2: “item 2”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">augmentation 1</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">no</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Text 2: “item 1”</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Text 1: “item 2”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">augmentation 2</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">no</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Text 1: “item 2”</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Text 2: “item 1”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">augmentation 3</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Text 2: “item 2”</td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Text 1: “item 1”</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given this prompt format, our in-context learning approach consists of three steps: augmentation, prompting, and calibration.
We first augment our pair-level data by performing two augmentation operations on every pair illustrated in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.2 Pairwise debiasing ‣ Appendix A Methodological details ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
In the prompting step, we then present one text pair at a time to the model, tasking it to respond with the label of the text item that is higher on the given comparison dimension (e.g., more emotionally intense).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we generate and record the model’s response, including its token probabilities for the two tokens “1” and “2.”
Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.2 Pairwise debiasing ‣ Appendix A Methodological details ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for an example of an LLM’s response and token probabilities for the original and augmentation versions of a text pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we debias the model’s response as follows.
Let <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability for the token corresponding to label “1” in the model’s generated response.
Let <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> indicate whether the labels were swapped (augmentations 1 and 3), and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> whether text items’ order was reversed (augmentations 2 and 3), where <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> indicating no swapping/reversing.
The model’s preference score for the first text item in a pair is <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, the model’s preference for the first item in a pair is captured by <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{00}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{00}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-p_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-p_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{01}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-p_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-p_{11}</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, given our augmentation strategy, which text item corresponds to choice “1” depends on whether the labels were swapped:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{ij}=\begin{cases}p_{ij},&amp;\text{if }i=0\text{ (i.e., {swapped})}\\
1-p_{ij},&amp;\text{if }i=1\text{ (i.e., not {swapped})}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> (i.e., </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">swapped</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> (i.e., not </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">swapped</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{ij}=\begin{cases}p_{ij},&amp;\text{if }i=0\text{ (i.e., {swapped})}\\
1-p_{ij},&amp;\text{if }i=1\text{ (i.e., not {swapped})}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The <em id="A1.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">debiased preference score</em> for the first text item, denoted <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the average of the <em id="A1.SS2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aligned</em> preferences across the original pair and the three augmented conditions:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{1}=\frac{1}{4}\sum_{i=0}^{1}\sum_{j=0}^{1}y_{ij}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{1}=\frac{1}{4}\sum_{i=0}^{1}\sum_{j=0}^{1}y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The corresponding debiased preference for the second item i <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{2}=1-\hat{p}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{2}=1-\hat{p}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, for the pair shown in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.2 Pairwise debiasing ‣ Appendix A Methodological details ‣ Measuring Scalar Constructs in Social Science with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the debiased preference scores are <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{1}=0.688" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.688</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{1}=0.688</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{2}=0.312" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.312</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{2}=0.312</annotation></semantics></math>.
The model’s debiased choice is therefore “1”.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Illustration of LLM’s preferences for response options and actual response (“choice”) for different augmentations of the same text pair (“original”).
</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Model</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" colspan="2">augmentations</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="2">token probs.</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">labels swapped</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">order reversed</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">“1”</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">“2”</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">choice</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><em id="A1.T5.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">original pair</em></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">no</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">no</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.996</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.004</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">“1”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">augmentation 1</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">no</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.699</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.301</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">“1”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">augmentation 3</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">no</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">yes</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.197</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.803</td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">“2”</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">augmentation 3</td>
